\date{2026-06-15T08:33:05Z}
\import{common}
\title{Recursively realizable one-variable formula in propositional logic}
\author{trebor}
\taxon{Question}
\p{
  What are all the propositional formulas involving one propositional variable that are realizable with recursive functions? In other words, what propositions are true in the computable world?
}

\p{
  Constructive logic is realizable with computable functions, and so we can restrict our attention to the free Heyting algebra generated by one element, also known as the [Rieger–Nishimura lattice](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rieger-Nishimura.svg), and asking if any proposition other than #{\top} is realizable. According to this [survey](https://www.jstor.org/stable/25470303), although there are propositions unprovable in constructive logic but realizable computably, the existence of such one-variable propositions is still open.
}
